## -l search (ImplementationPlan.md 5.10.9; 5.9.14), as M15 completes it:
## - the candidate names depend on the triple: lib<n>.a, <n>.lib, <n>.dll on
##   windows-msvc; lib<n>.a, <n>.dll, lib<n>.dll, lib<n>.so on ELF triples,
##   where the two DLL names are candidates only as .NET assemblies (a native
##   library is read only in the object format of the triple's OS, the
##   section 12 row of 2026-10-23); lib<n>.a, lib<n>.dll.a, <n>.dll.a,
##   <n>.lib, lib<n>.dll, <n>.dll on windows-gnu;
## - the -L directories are searched in order and the first one that holds an
##   acceptable candidate wins; inside it a static library (an archive with
##   bitcode members) wins over a native one (Design.md 5.2's static-first
##   rule, the opposite of GNU ld);
## - a native candidate (an import library, a DLL, a shared object or its
##   linker script) is read and classified, and binds the functions it
##   defines (M14 accepted it unread, and only D14's imports were bound):
##   puts binds to the UCRT's stdio API set, glibc's libc.so.6 (through the
##   real linker scripts, under --sysroot) and msvcrt.dll;
## - a native-only archive is skipped, and so is a .NET assembly, with the
##   warning '<path>: skipping the .NET assembly that -l<name> found; use
##   --reference for .NET reference assemblies' (M22 links toolchain
##   libraries); neither hides a later directory;
## - -Bstatic accepts static candidates only;
## - a candidate for another machine and a malformed one are errors;
## - -l:<file> takes that exact name from the -L directories;
## - anything else is 'unable to find library -l<name>'.
## The driver's default libraries (5.10.4) are found this way.
## (native-search.test has the full rules.)

